WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.800
Today we turn circuit measurements into physical conclusions.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.382
Start with a resistor, a low voltage supply, a current limiting arrangement,

00:00:10.382 --> 00:00:10.500
an

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.120
ammeter in series, and a voltmeter across the resistor.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.300
The ammeter follows the current through the circuit; the voltmeter compares

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.680
potential at two terminals.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.240
An ammeter must never be connected directly across the supply.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.212
In our ideal worked model, its resistance is negligible and the voltmeter

00:00:27.212 --> 00:00:27.500
draws

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.440
negligible current.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.340
Real instruments introduce loading, so these are assumptions, not promises.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.020
Change the current gently, recording corresponding voltage and current pairs.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.440
Keep the resistor's temperature approximately constant, because heating can

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.340
change its resistance.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.876
Suppose our original data are zero point one ampere at one volt, zero point

00:00:48.876 --> 00:00:49.060
two

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.920
at two volts, and zero point three at three volts.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.620
Voltage divided by current is ten ohms each time.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.520
Plot voltage vertically against current horizontally: the fitted slope is ten

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.560
volts per ampere, or ten ohms.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.120
Now a quick trap.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.940
What if current is vertical and voltage horizontal?

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.060
Pause and inspect the units.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.520
The slope is zero point one ampere per volt, the conductance, not the

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.020
resistance.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.260
Take its reciprocal.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.729
A straight line through the corrected origin supports Ohm's law over the

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:20.040
tested

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.600
range and conditions; it does not prove every device is ohmic.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:29.360
Use several observations, sensible ranges, short observation times, and a

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.300
supervised low voltage setup.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:34.280
A single voltage current pair cannot establish a linear relationship.

00:01:35.016 --> 00:01:39.536
A metre bridge measures resistance by finding a balance, rather than measuring

00:01:39.536 --> 00:01:41.196
a large galvanometer current.

00:01:42.056 --> 00:01:46.436
Put unknown resistance X in the left gap and known resistance K in the right

00:01:46.436 --> 00:01:46.636
gap.

00:01:47.536 --> 00:01:50.636
Connect the supply across the ends of the uniform bridge wire.

00:01:51.196 --> 00:01:55.596
A sensitive galvanometer joins the gap junction to a gently applied jockey

00:01:55.596 --> 00:01:56.736
contact on that wire.

00:01:57.536 --> 00:02:01.656
At the null point, those two junctions have equal potential, so no current

00:02:01.656 --> 00:02:02.916
passes through the galvanometer.

00:02:03.576 --> 00:02:06.196
Current still flows through the other bridge branches.

00:02:07.396 --> 00:02:08.856
Why does length appear in the answer?

00:02:09.676 --> 00:02:14.196
The two sections of the uniform wire have the same material and area, so their

00:02:14.196 --> 00:02:16.236
resistances are proportional to their lengths.

00:02:17.096 --> 00:02:21.746
The balanced ratios therefore give X divided by K equals l divided by one

00:02:21.746 --> 00:02:26.096
hundred minus l, when l is measured in centimeters from the left end of an

00:02:26.096 --> 00:02:27.696
exactly one hundred centimeter wire.

00:02:28.516 --> 00:02:33.236
Our example has K equal to two ohms and l equal to sixty centimeters.

00:02:34.076 --> 00:02:37.576
The unknown is two times sixty over forty, giving three ohms.

00:02:38.556 --> 00:02:41.796
Before memorizing anything, point to the left resistor and its corresponding

00:02:41.796 --> 00:02:42.756
left wire length.

00:02:43.876 --> 00:02:47.616
If you swap X and K, the ideal balance moves to forty centimeters.

00:02:48.256 --> 00:02:52.956
The unknown is now in the right gap, so X equals K times one hundred minus l,

00:02:53.276 --> 00:02:54.016
divided by l.

00:02:54.696 --> 00:02:56.216
That again gives three ohms.

00:02:56.636 --> 00:03:00.116
Use the actual wire length if it is not exactly one hundred centimeters.

00:03:01.056 --> 00:03:04.715
A formula without a clearly defined gap arrangement is an invitation to

00:03:04.715 --> 00:03:05.076
reverse

00:03:05.076 --> 00:03:05.496
the answer.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.640
Resistance describes this particular wire.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.360
Resistivity describes its material at the stated temperature.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:17.080
To move from one to the other, we need the test wire's length and cross

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:17.760
sectional area.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:23.242
For a uniform circular wire, resistance equals resistivity times length

00:03:23.242 --> 00:03:23.680
divided

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:24.300
by area.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.960
Rearranging gives rho equals X times pi d squared divided by four L.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.520
Here capital L means the length of the test wire between its effective

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:35.900
terminals.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.140
It is not the sixty centimeter bridge balancing length from our previous

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:40.600
example.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:45.490
Suppose that three ohm test wire is one point two meters long and has

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:45.940
corrected

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.160
diameter zero point four millimeters.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.860
First convert diameter to zero point zero zero zero four meters.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.640
Its area is pi times that diameter squared divided by four.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.460
Multiply by three ohms and divide by one point two meters.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.700
The resistivity is about three point one four times ten to the minus seven ohm

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:07.060
meters.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.400
Radius and diameter cannot be swapped without changing the area by a factor of

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:12.600
four.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.720
Measure diameter at multiple places and orientations to check uniformity.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.720
Keep contacts clean and use brief observations to limit heating.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:26.920
For the bridge, choose known resistances that place the null point near the

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:31.000
middle; ratios become more vulnerable to length errors near the ends.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.960
Interchanging gap positions helps reveal and reduce some end effects, but does

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.260
not magically remove every systematic error.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:43.100
Nonuniform bridge wire and significant contact resistances still matter.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.780
A tidy average cannot fix a wrong physical model.

00:04:46.992 --> 00:04:51.772
Now we measure the resistance of a sensitive galvanometer without sending a

00:04:51.772 --> 00:04:52.492
dangerous current through it.

00:04:53.112 --> 00:04:57.372
A large series resistance R limits current from a stable low voltage source.

00:04:58.232 --> 00:05:02.652
With the parallel shunt disconnected, note an initial deflection theta within

00:05:02.652 --> 00:05:04.072
the instrument's linear range.

00:05:05.012 --> 00:05:10.089
Keep R fixed, connect a shunt S across the galvanometer, and adjust S until

00:05:10.089 --> 00:05:10.292
the

00:05:10.292 --> 00:05:12.032
deflection is half its original value.

00:05:12.992 --> 00:05:15.952
Linear deflection means the galvanometer current has halved.

00:05:16.532 --> 00:05:20.892
Here is the subtle point: the total supply current has not stayed exactly

00:05:20.892 --> 00:05:21.312
constant.

00:05:22.352 --> 00:05:24.852
Adding a parallel branch changes the circuit resistance.

00:05:25.732 --> 00:05:29.612
With the shunt open, galvanometer current is E divided by R plus G.

00:05:30.332 --> 00:05:34.632
With the shunt connected, current division gives galvanometer current E S

00:05:34.632 --> 00:05:38.452
divided by R times G plus S, plus G S.

00:05:39.332 --> 00:05:41.672
Set the second expression equal to half the first.

00:05:41.672 --> 00:05:46.132
After cancellation, R G equals R S plus G S.

00:05:47.152 --> 00:05:50.272
Rearranging gives G equals R S divided by R minus S.

00:05:50.992 --> 00:05:55.612
Our original example uses R equal to nine thousand nine hundred ohms and S

00:05:55.612 --> 00:05:56.832
equal to ninety nine ohms.

00:05:57.452 --> 00:06:00.412
The result is exactly one hundred ohms in this ideal model.

00:06:01.592 --> 00:06:04.832
Simply calling G ninety nine ohms would use an approximation.

00:06:05.672 --> 00:06:09.912
When R is much larger than S, the correction becomes small and G is

00:06:09.912 --> 00:06:10.792
approximately S.

00:06:11.332 --> 00:06:14.932
For this example it underestimates G by one percent.

00:06:14.932 --> 00:06:18.932
Start with a high current limiting resistance and never connect an unprotected

00:06:18.932 --> 00:06:20.892
galvanometer directly to a supply.

00:06:22.092 --> 00:06:26.111
Battery internal resistance is neglected here; if it matters, include it in

00:06:26.111 --> 00:06:26.272
the

00:06:26.272 --> 00:06:28.232
total series resistance used by the model.

00:06:28.704 --> 00:06:33.864
A galvanometer's figure of merit tells us how much current is required for one

00:06:33.864 --> 00:06:34.524
scale division.

00:06:35.184 --> 00:06:38.484
It is not its resistance, and it is not the deflection per ampere.

00:06:39.264 --> 00:06:41.444
Let k be current divided by deflection.

00:06:42.404 --> 00:06:47.854
For the unshunted circuit, k equals E divided by the product of R plus G and

00:06:47.854 --> 00:06:48.284
theta.

00:06:49.124 --> 00:06:52.284
Use the original full deflection, measured with the shunt disconnected.

00:06:52.984 --> 00:06:58.404
Suppose E is two volts, R is nine thousand nine hundred ohms, G is one hundred

00:06:58.404 --> 00:07:01.224
ohms, and the original deflection is forty divisions.

00:07:02.624 --> 00:07:06.804
The current is two divided by ten thousand amperes: two hundred microamperes.

00:07:07.624 --> 00:07:10.464
Divide by forty divisions to get five microamperes per division.

00:07:11.464 --> 00:07:15.714
If the same linear instrument later receives seventy five microamperes, how

00:07:15.714 --> 00:07:15.884
far

00:07:15.884 --> 00:07:16.484
should it deflect?

00:07:17.384 --> 00:07:18.584
Give yourself a moment.

00:07:18.584 --> 00:07:21.304
Current divided by k gives fifteen divisions.

00:07:22.224 --> 00:07:26.084
Smaller current per division means greater sensitivity, because less current

00:07:26.084 --> 00:07:27.744
produces the same visible deflection.

00:07:28.624 --> 00:07:32.950
The reciprocal, deflection per current, is the current sensitivity; do not

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:33.184
swap

00:07:33.184 --> 00:07:33.724
these units.

00:07:34.724 --> 00:07:38.624
In an observation table, record the series resistance, original deflection,

00:07:39.184 --> 00:07:41.364
half deflection, and shunt resistance together.

00:07:42.264 --> 00:07:46.278
Calculate G for each valid set, then use the unshunted readings to calculate

00:07:46.278 --> 00:07:46.384
k.

00:07:46.944 --> 00:07:51.684
Check the pointer's zero, avoid parallax, keep connections reliable, and stay

00:07:51.684 --> 00:07:52.984
within safe deflections.

00:07:53.904 --> 00:07:57.909
Repetition reveals scatter, but supply drift, wrong zero and instrument

00:07:57.909 --> 00:07:58.304
loading

00:07:58.304 --> 00:07:59.564
need separate attention.

00:08:00.304 --> 00:08:04.444
Our numbers are original teaching examples, not measurements we performed or

00:08:04.444 --> 00:08:05.884
official past paper questions.

00:08:06.744 --> 00:08:11.284
The JEE skill is connecting the circuit arrangement, observation and justified

00:08:11.284 --> 00:08:14.604
equation, not memorizing an isolated laboratory formula.
